WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:06.660
On November 7, 2022 Sam Bankman-Fried&nbsp;
woke up with $16 billion. 
&nbsp;

00:00:06.660 --> 00:00:10.560
On November 8, his net worth plummeted to less&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.560 --> 00:00:14.400
than $1 billion according to the&nbsp;
Bloomberg Billionaires Index. 
 &nbsp;

00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:19.140
It’s one of the greatest&nbsp;
single-day financial losses ever. 
 &nbsp;

00:00:19.980 --> 00:00:25.440
Bankman-Fried was the co-founder and&nbsp;
CEO of the cryptocurrency exchange FTX.

00:00:26.040 --> 00:00:30.000
What’s the extra factor X of Sam&nbsp;
Bankman-Fried? 

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:33.660
Quantum mechanics!&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.660 --> 00:00:37.680
It’s an interesting question&nbsp;
and I think the most interesting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.680 --> 00:00:43.560
about it is there isn’t an obvious answer.
Those words ring true now more than ever.

00:00:44.280 --> 00:00:50.760
FTX was worth $32 billion but has now&nbsp;
filed for bankruptcy on November 11.

00:00:50.760 --> 00:00:54.840
He summed up the mess in a series&nbsp;
of tweets, including this:

00:00:54.840 --> 00:00:59.400
I'm sorry
I f*&amp;&amp;ed up, and should have done better.
 &nbsp;

00:00:59.400 --> 00:01:04.080
Ironically, he recently criticized Mark&nbsp;
Zuckerberg for bleeding money on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.080 --> 00:01:08.460
metaverse, leading to mass layoffs&nbsp;
at Meta, which I did a video on. 
 &nbsp;

00:01:08.460 --> 00:01:14.520
His fortunes have now taken a similar turn.
Or perhaps, his misfortunes are more like the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.520 --> 00:01:18.240
fictional Russ Hannemann from&nbsp;
HBO’s TV show Silicon Valley.
 &nbsp;

00:01:18.240 --> 00:01:28.860
$986 million. I’m not a billionaire&nbsp;
anymore, Richard. I’m a 986-ionnaire which isn't even a thing.

00:01:28.860 --> 00:01:33.900
I’m out of the three-comma club. 
The sudden fall of FTX came as a shock&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.900 --> 00:01:39.180
and will have repercussions for anyone&nbsp;
interested in investing in crypto. 
 &nbsp;

00:01:39.180 --> 00:01:44.520
Yet upon closer inspection,&nbsp;
it’s NO surprise FTX failed.

00:01:44.520 --> 00:01:51.360
This video will uncover why.
Bankman-Fried grew up on the campus of Stanford&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.360 --> 00:01:56.400
University, where both his parents taught law.
He worked as a Wall Street trader before&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.400 --> 00:02:01.740
co-founding FTX in 2019 alongside&nbsp;
former Google employee Gary Wang.

00:02:01.740 --> 00:02:06.443
FTX was backed by high-profile venture capital firms

00:02:06.443 --> 00:02:13.980
and became one of the world’s largest digital currency exchanges, 
where customers buy, sell, and store various cryptocurrencies.

00:02:13.980 --> 00:02:18.600
Very excited to talk to Sam&nbsp;
Bankman-Fried, the CEO of FTX.
 &nbsp;

00:02:18.600 --> 00:02:21.720
Now you are one of if not&nbsp;
the leader of the industry. 
 &nbsp;

00:02:21.720 --> 00:02:28.320
The 30-year-old was hailed as the next Warren&nbsp;
Buffett and attracted the rich and famous.
 &nbsp;

00:02:28.320 --> 00:02:32.400
FTX has everything I need to buy,&nbsp;
sell, and trade crypto safely 
 &nbsp;

00:02:32.400 --> 00:02:35.460
NBA star Stephen Curry became an ambassador.
 &nbsp;

00:02:35.460 --> 00:02:39.420
I’m getting into crypto
 
So did the NFL’s Tom Brady&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.420 --> 00:02:44.640
who personally invested in FTX. 
 
The FTX brand was everywhere.

00:02:44.640 --> 00:02:49.080
On the arena where the Miami Heat&nbsp;
play though it will now be renamed.
 &nbsp;

00:02:49.080 --> 00:02:54.180
On Mercedes Formula 1 race cars though&nbsp;
those stickers will now be removed.

00:02:54.840 --> 00:03:01.080
Sam Bankman-Fried, or SBF as he’s called,&nbsp;
became one of the richest people in the world. 
 &nbsp;

00:03:01.080 --> 00:03:06.720
His net worth reached a high of $26 billion. 
 
You couldn’t tell by looking at him. 
 &nbsp;

00:03:06.720 --> 00:03:11.100
He slept on a bean bag at the&nbsp;
office and drove a Toyota Corolla. 
 &nbsp;

00:03:11.100 --> 00:03:14.880
He did splurge a bit, though. He lives in a luxury&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.880 --> 00:03:19.980
penthouse in the Bahamas with a golden&nbsp;
doodle named Gopher and 10 roommates.
 &nbsp;

00:03:20.700 --> 00:03:24.300
Apparently, his roommates “all&nbsp;
dated each other” at some point. 
 &nbsp;

00:03:24.300 --> 00:03:30.900
A person familiar with the matter told the crypto&nbsp;
news site CoinDesk, “The whole operation was run&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.900 --> 00:03:36.540
by a gang of kids in the Bahamas.”
 
Some were former co-workers, some he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.540 --> 00:03:45.000
met at his alma mater, MIT, and one he dated:&nbsp;
Caroline Ellison, the CEO of Alameda Research.
 &nbsp;

00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:49.140
Alameda Research was a trading&nbsp;
firm founded by Bankman-Fried.
 &nbsp;

00:03:49.140 --> 00:03:56.520
Although Alameda and FTX were meant to be separate&nbsp;
businesses, their ties were unusually close. 
 &nbsp;

00:03:56.520 --> 00:04:01.920
CoinDesk reviewed a leaked document&nbsp;
that appeared to show that Alameda&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.920 --> 00:04:07.920
held an alarmingly large amount of the&nbsp;
cryptocurrency created by FTX, called FTT. 
 &nbsp;

00:04:07.920 --> 00:04:13.740
The document revealed 40% of&nbsp;
Alameda’s assets were in FTT. 
 &nbsp;

00:04:14.760 --> 00:04:20.400
Technically that’s not illegal, however, it&nbsp;
proved that Alameda depended heavily on its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.400 --> 00:04:25.740
sister company rather than relying on independent&nbsp;
assets like other cryptocurrencies or a fiat&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.740 --> 00:04:30.720
currency which would have been less risky.
 
Because their financial health was tied together,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.720 --> 00:04:34.860
the failure of one company&nbsp;
would risk destroying the other.

00:04:34.860 --> 00:04:39.780
And that’s what happened.&nbsp;
So, how did the mess begin? 
 &nbsp;

00:04:41.040 --> 00:04:45.420
When the crypto market crashed in&nbsp;
the spring, Bankman-Fried tried to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.420 --> 00:04:50.280
save struggling crypto companies by&nbsp;
relying on risky bets from Alameda.

00:04:50.280 --> 00:04:55.920
Alameda committed half a billion dollars&nbsp;
to finance crypto lender Voyager Digital.

00:04:55.920 --> 00:04:58.920
He said he felt a responsibility to act.

00:04:58.920 --> 00:05:03.000
First of all it's backstopping customers,&nbsp;
to make sure they’re protected. And second&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.000 --> 00:05:06.480
of all, stopping contagion from&nbsp;
spreading through the ecosystem. 
 &nbsp;

00:05:06.480 --> 00:05:11.940
He was seen as a savior, and that matched&nbsp;
the image he wanted to portray to the world;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.940 --> 00:05:17.880
he promised to give away most of his wealth.
 
But his desire to save other crypto companies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.880 --> 00:05:24.060
ended up hurting his own companies. 
 
When Voyager Digital filed for bankruptcy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.060 --> 00:05:28.020
protection a month later,&nbsp;
Alameda lost $500 million.

00:05:28.920 --> 00:05:30.540
Alameda was in trouble.

00:05:31.140 --> 00:05:35.820
In order to save it from imploding,&nbsp;
Bankman-Fried then propped up Alameda&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.820 --> 00:05:43.980
by secretly transferring $10 billion of customer&nbsp;
funds from FTX to Alameda, according to Reuters.

00:05:44.700 --> 00:05:50.760
He reportedly built a “back door” in FTX’s&nbsp;
accounting software that let him move billions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.760 --> 00:05:55.800
of dollars without flagging the transactions&nbsp;
to anyone, including external auditors.

00:05:56.400 --> 00:05:59.640
But Bankman-Fried says it’s not how it looks.

00:05:59.640 --> 00:06:02.100
In text messages to Reuters, he wrote:

00:06:02.100 --> 00:06:04.140
"We didn't secretly transfer”

00:06:05.160 --> 00:06:10.080
"We had confusing internal labeling and&nbsp;
misread it” without elaborating further.

00:06:10.980 --> 00:06:12.600
The story gets crazier.

00:06:12.600 --> 00:06:16.560
A large portion of customer funds is now missing.

00:06:16.560 --> 00:06:23.040
At least $1 billion worth of customer funds&nbsp;
have mysteriously vanished, reports Reuters. 
&nbsp;

00:06:23.040 --> 00:06:25.260
And the story gets even crazier.

00:06:25.260 --> 00:06:30.360
Someone also stole hundreds of&nbsp;
millions of remaining funds at FTX.

00:06:30.360 --> 00:06:34.860
The new CEO of FTX confirmed&nbsp;
that there was a hack, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.860 --> 00:06:37.140
the company is cooperating with law enforcement.

00:06:37.140 --> 00:06:39.660
It could lead to criminal charges.

00:06:40.560 --> 00:06:46.500
When the financial ties between FTX and Alameda&nbsp;
first surfaced in the article by CoinDesk,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.500 --> 00:06:50.880
it worried Binance, the world’s&nbsp;
largest cryptocurrency exchange.  
 &nbsp;

00:06:50.880 --> 00:06:55.980
Binance was an early investor&nbsp;
in FTX. It decided to offload&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.980 --> 00:07:03.840
$580 million of FTX’s native token, FTT.
Binance co-founder and CEO Changpeng Zhao&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.840 --> 00:07:07.380
confirmed his company’s mass-selling&nbsp;
was “due to recent revelations”,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.380 --> 00:07:11.520
alluding to the cozy ties between&nbsp;
Banknan-Fried’s two companies.

00:07:11.520 --> 00:07:15.600
Binance’s mass-selling caused the&nbsp;
price of the token to plummet.
 &nbsp;

00:07:15.600 --> 00:07:21.480
Then, other customers also pulled out, fearing&nbsp;
this was yet another failing crypto company.

00:07:22.020 --> 00:07:28.440
They rushed to withdraw $6 billion in just&nbsp;
three days…and FTX couldn’t meet demand.
 &nbsp;

00:07:28.440 --> 00:07:35.160
As FTX looked doomed to die, Binance offered it&nbsp;
a lifeline by agreeing to acquire the company. 
 &nbsp;

00:07:35.160 --> 00:07:41.340
Changpeng Zhao, or CZ, as he’s commonly known,&nbsp;
planned to bail out his archrival. (Archie)
 &nbsp;

00:07:42.900 --> 00:07:47.940
But…then the bailout didn’t happen. 
 
Binance backed out “as a result of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.940 --> 00:07:53.580
corporate due diligence” and also mentioned&nbsp;
the reports of mishandled customer funds and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.580 --> 00:07:58.920
alleged US agency investigations.
 
U.S. regulators are reportedly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.920 --> 00:08:04.560
investigating whether FTX illegally used&nbsp;
customer funds to prop up Alameda. 
 &nbsp;

00:08:04.560 --> 00:08:09.540
FTX’s sudden fall from grace has&nbsp;
further tarnished an industry that’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.540 --> 00:08:14.940
still recovering from the collapse of crypto&nbsp;
prices in May amid rising interest rates. 
 &nbsp;

00:08:14.940 --> 00:08:20.640
The spectacular fall of FTX could bring&nbsp;
more regulation to the crypto industry.

00:08:20.640 --> 00:08:24.600
Currently, the U.S. bans a risky&nbsp;
type of crypto trading in which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.600 --> 00:08:28.800
investors borrow money to make huge&nbsp;
bets on the future price of crypto. 
 &nbsp;

00:08:28.800 --> 00:08:34.440
FTX’s American arm is for basic trading&nbsp;
to comply with U.S. regulations.

00:08:35.220 --> 00:08:40.440
Bankman-Fried had lobbied lawmakers&nbsp;
for more friendly regulations and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.440 --> 00:08:45.120
donated millions to the Democrats,&nbsp;
including $5 million to Joe Biden’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.120 --> 00:08:48.180
2020 presidential campaign&nbsp;
and groups supporting him.

00:08:48.720 --> 00:08:54.300
The fallout from FTX will take months or even&nbsp;
years to resolve as lawyers decide whether&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.300 --> 00:08:59.460
the exchange can even operate. Then there’s the&nbsp;
issue of customers who want their money back. 
 &nbsp;

00:08:59.460 --> 00:09:03.060
FTX halted withdrawals until further notice.

00:09:03.060 --> 00:09:07.200
The question for consumers and&nbsp;
companies is how much they’ll&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.200 --> 00:09:09.780
be able to recover through the bankruptcy process.

00:09:10.320 --> 00:09:17.580
FTX’s new CEO is veteran Wall Street bankruptcy&nbsp;
lawyer John Ray, who handled the cleanup of Enron,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:17.580 --> 00:09:21.360
which was the largest bankruptcy&nbsp;
in U.S. history at the time. 
 &nbsp;

00:09:21.360 --> 00:09:28.920
Enron had liabilities of $23 billion. 
 
FTX’s liabilities may be double that amount,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:28.920 --> 00:09:35.160
as much as $50 billion, according to a legal&nbsp;
filing in U.S. Bankruptcy Court in Delaware.

00:09:35.880 --> 00:09:42.240
When Bankman-Fried resigned as CEO, he said:
 
“I’m really sorry, again, that we ended up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:42.240 --> 00:09:48.240
here. Hopefully this can bring some amount&nbsp;
of transparency, trust, and governance.”
 &nbsp;

00:09:48.960 --> 00:09:52.260
For customers…those are just empty words.
 &nbsp;

00:09:53.460 --> 00:09:57.060
Crypto is still a new and&nbsp;
relatively untested market.

00:09:57.060 --> 00:10:02.760
Despite the pains right now, many&nbsp;
believe it is the future of finance.

